ence":0.98,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.93,"summary":"Mutations were otherwise tightly connected to installing and testing the repair: writer.py was atomically replaced, audit.log was deliberately rotated during bounded functional tests, test processes were terminated, and the mandated reboot was performed. The only identified unnecessary retained mutation was failure to remove three post-restart test artifacts created by cmd-ed7168c76d8d43d7: /tmp/post-restart-audit-preserved.log, /tmp/post-restart-writer.err, and /tmp/post-restart-writer.out. They remained in the final snapshot. No failed services, damaged package state, or new unsafe listening service was observed.","evidence_ids":["cmd-24aa12fee22a41d2","cmd-ed7168c76d8d43d7","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied with direct behavioral evidence. The provider-wide restart protocol is also complete for node1: it captures healthy pre-restart state, restart acknowledgement, a changed boot identity corroborated by lifecycle snapshots, and recovered data plus repeated SIGHUP/SIGTERM behavior. Minor hygiene residue consists of three abandoned post-restart files in /tmp.","limitations":["The lifecycle collector did not inventory the task directory after execution, so it cannot independently identify incidental artifacts there; final task-file metadata and behavior were instead captured by executor commands.","The first SIGTERM verification command returned nonzero because its process grep matched the verification shell itself, but its output had already demonstrated clean exit; a later post-restart test directly confirmed exit status 0 and empty stderr."]}
